From cd1863c749aae4b92c8c348a3db030acea1f4240 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunshineinabox <aqemail@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 03:14:34 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] freedreno: bypass GMEM for uncleared one- or two-draw passes

A batch with nothing cleared has GMEM load every attachment into every
bin and store it again, on top of replaying its draws per bin. One or
two draws that pass about one sample per pixel (a full-screen pass) read
and write less than that in sysmem, but the autotune's draw cost grows
with the samples passed, so such a pass always went to GMEM.

Megabonk (Unity) on A610: a one-draw pass over its 1280x720 RGBA16F +
Z24S8 target, depth test on and nothing cleared, took 6.1 ms in 90 bins,
0.9 ms of it loading color, depth and stencil.

Use sysmem for a batch with history, nothing cleared, at most two draws
and at most 1.5 samples passed per pixel.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_autotune.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_autotune.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_autotune.c
index aa8bf9afdcb..8543c7b64ad 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_autotune.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_autotune.c
@@ -208,6 +208,17 @@ fd_autotune_use_bypass(struct fd_autotune *at, struct fd_batch *batch)
       if (avg_samples < 500.0f)
          return true;
 
+      /* Nothing cleared means GMEM loads every attachment into every bin
+       * and stores it again, on top of replaying the draws per bin. One or
+       * two draws that touch each pixel about once (a full-screen pass)
+       * read and write less than that in sysmem: Megabonk's one-draw pass
+       * over its 1280x720 RGBA16F + Z24S8 target took 6.1 ms in 90 bins on
+       * A610, the loads alone 0.9 ms.
+       */
+      if (!batch->cleared && batch->num_draws <= 2 &&
+          avg_samples <= 1.5f * pfb->width * pfb->height)
+         return true;
+
       /* Cost-per-sample is an estimate for the average number of reads+
        * writes for a given passed sample.
        */
-- 
2.56.1

